Cordon command in k8S?
Marks a node as unschedulable — new pods won’t be placed there.
kubectl cordon node1
To allow scheduling again: kubectl uncordon node1

Diff between cordon and taint?
kubectl cordon marks a node as unschedulable, so no new pods will be scheduled there, but existing pods keep running. It’s usually used during maintenance.
kubectl taint applies a rule to a node so only pods with matching tolerations can run on it.
Taints provide more fine-grained control and are used to dedicate or isolate nodes for specific workloads.

